-- ============================================================
-- ADVANCED BASIS CALCULATIONS (3 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 4205: Advanced Basis Calculations
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 732(b), a partner''s basis in property received in a liquidating distribution equals the partner''s outside basis in the partnership interest. When multiple properties are distributed and the outside basis ($50,000) is less than total inside basis ($70,000), Section 732(c) requires the $20,000 decrease to be allocated first to unrealized depreciation, then proportionally by adjusted basis. Applying proportional allocation: Property 1 receives $50,000 × ($30,000/$70,000) = $21,429 and Property 2 receives $50,000 × ($40,000/$70,000) = $28,571. Unlike current distributions under Section 732(a), which preserve inside basis, liquidating distributions force basis to match the surrendered outside basis.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4205;

-- ID 4206: Advanced Basis Calculations
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 752(a), a partner''s share of partnership liabilities increases outside basis. When a nonrecourse liability is converted to recourse, Section 752 requires reallocation based on economic risk of loss under Reg. Section 1.752-2. Before conversion, Partner M''s share of the $60,000 nonrecourse liability was $20,000 (one-third). After conversion with M bearing all economic risk, M''s share becomes $60,000, a net increase of $40,000. New outside basis = $120,000 + $40,000 = $160,000. Unlike nonrecourse liabilities shared under Section 752(c) rules based on profits interests, recourse liabilities under Section 752(b) are allocated solely to the partner bearing the economic risk of loss.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4206;

-- ID 4207: Advanced Basis Calculations
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 1367(a)(1), S corporation income increases the shareholder''s stock basis. When prior losses have reduced both stock and debt basis to zero, Section 1367(b)(2)(B) requires that income first restores debt basis that was previously reduced by losses, then increases stock basis. Since no debt basis was reduced here (debt basis was already $0 without prior reduction), the full $15,000 of allocated income increases stock basis directly to $15,000. Unlike partnership outside basis under Section 705, S corporation basis separately tracks stock and debt components with a specific restoration ordering.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4207;

-- ============================================================
-- CAPITAL STRUCTURE TAX PLANNING (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 4341: Capital Structure Tax Planning
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 163(j), the business interest deduction is limited to 30% of adjusted taxable income (ATI). For tax years beginning after December 31, 2025, Section 163(j)(8)(A)(v) provides that ATI is computed after subtracting depreciation, amortization, and depletion — a significant change from earlier years when these deductions were added back to ATI. This results in a lower ATI for capital-intensive businesses, reducing the 30% limitation and potentially disallowing more interest expense. Unlike the pre-2026 computation that added back depreciation and amortization, the post-2025 rule produces a substantially tighter interest limitation for asset-heavy taxpayers.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4341;

-- ID 4342: Capital Structure Tax Planning
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Sections 512 through 514, when a tax-exempt organization acquires debt-financed property, the income attributable to that property constitutes unrelated business taxable income (UBTI). Section 514(a) defines debt-financed property as any income-producing property with acquisition indebtedness. The interest expense on the borrowed funds is deductible only against the UBTI generated by that debt-financed property under Section 512(a)(1), not against the organization''s exempt function income. Unlike income from activities substantially related to the organization''s exempt purpose, debt-financed rental income is subject to UBIT regardless of how the proceeds are used.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4342;

-- ============================================================
-- ENTITY CHOICE AND PLANNING (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 4303: Entity Choice and Planning
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 162(a)(1), compensation is deductible only if it is reasonable in amount. When a C corporation pays its sole shareholder-employee $400,000 for a position where comparable compensation is $200,000, the IRS may recharacterize the $200,000 excess as a constructive dividend under the multi-factor reasonableness test established in case law. The corporation loses the deduction for the excess amount because dividends are not deductible under Section 162, creating double taxation — once at the corporate level and again as dividend income to the shareholder. Unlike reasonable salary which is deductible to the corporation, constructive dividends provide no corporate deduction while still being taxable to the recipient.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4303;

-- ID 4304: Entity Choice and Planning
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 761(f), a qualified joint venture allows spouses who jointly own and operate an unincorporated business to elect out of partnership treatment when filing jointly. Each spouse reports their share of income and expenses on a separate Schedule C, avoiding the requirement to file Form 1065. This simplifies compliance while allowing each spouse to receive Social Security earnings credit under FICA for their respective share. Unlike a partnership, which requires its own information return and K-1 schedules, a qualified joint venture treats each spouse as a sole proprietor for federal tax purposes.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4304;

-- ============================================================
-- ENTITY FORMATION AND RESTRUCTURING (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 5003: Entity Formation and Restructuring
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 338(h)(10), a buyer who acquires a target corporation''s stock can elect to treat the purchase as a deemed asset sale, obtaining a stepped-up basis in the target''s assets. The stock sale is disregarded, and only the deemed asset sale is recognized on the old target''s final return. This avoids the double taxation that would occur in a direct asset purchase — corporate-level gain on the asset sale plus shareholder-level gain on liquidation. Unlike a regular Section 338 election that triggers two levels of tax, the 338(h)(10) election is particularly advantageous when the target is an S corporation or consolidated subsidiary, as gain flows through to shareholders who offset it with stock basis.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5003;

-- ID 13580: Entity Formation and Restructuring
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRS Revenue Procedure 77-37, the ''substantially all'' assets requirement for a Type C reorganization under IRC Section 368(a)(1)(C) is met when the transferor conveys at least 90% of the fair market value of net assets and at least 70% of the fair market value of gross assets. Pine Corp''s transfer of 85% gross and 90% net exceeds both safe harbor thresholds, satisfying the requirement. Unlike a Type A reorganization where there is no explicit statutory ''substantially all'' requirement, Type C reorganizations impose this quantitative test to ensure meaningful asset transfers. The built-in gain status of retained assets is irrelevant — the test is purely based on fair market value percentages.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13580;

-- ============================================================
-- ENTITY TAX COMPLIANCE: NOLS AND CONSOLIDATED RETURNS (5 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 4091: Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 172(a) as amended by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act, the net operating loss deduction is limited to 80% of taxable income for losses arising in tax years beginning after December 31, 2017. For a consolidated group with $400,000 of consolidated taxable income before the CNOL deduction, the maximum deduction is $400,000 × 80% = $320,000. The remaining $280,000 of the $600,000 CNOL carries forward indefinitely under Section 172(b)(1)(A)(ii). Unlike pre-TCJA rules that allowed NOLs to offset 100% of taxable income, the 80% limitation ensures the group pays tax on at least 20% of its pre-NOL income.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4091;

-- ID 4092: Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under Reg. Section 1.1502-13(c), the matching rule governs intercompany transactions by deferring the selling member''s gain until the buying member has a corresponding item with an unrelated party. Sub A''s deferred intercompany gain of $150,000 ($250,000 − $100,000) is triggered when Sub B sells the land externally. Sub B recognizes $50,000 ($300,000 − $250,000), and Sub A''s $150,000 deferred gain is simultaneously recognized, producing total consolidated gain of $200,000 ($300,000 − $100,000 original basis). Unlike transactions with unrelated parties where gain is immediately recognized, the matching rule preserves single-entity treatment until an external event occurs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4092;

-- ID 4093: Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under Reg. Section 1.1502-13(d), the acceleration rule requires immediate recognition of any remaining deferred intercompany gain or loss when either the selling or buying member leaves the consolidated group. When Beta is sold to an unrelated buyer in 2025, Beta''s departure triggers acceleration of the $200,000 deferred intercompany gain from the 2023 inventory sale. This prevents deferred gains from permanently escaping consolidated group taxation. Unlike the matching rule under Reg. Section 1.1502-13(c), which triggers recognition upon a corresponding external transaction, the acceleration rule triggers recognition based on a change in group membership regardless of whether the property has been sold externally.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4093;

-- ID 4094: Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under Reg. Section 1.1502-21(c), the separate return limitation year (SRLY) rules limit a new member''s pre-acquisition NOL to its cumulative contribution to consolidated taxable income. Theta''s contribution is $50,000, which caps the usable NOL. The Section 172(a) 80% limitation further reduces the deduction to $50,000 × 80% = $40,000. The remaining $60,000 carries forward subject to continued SRLY limitations. Unlike consolidated NOLs (CNOLs) generated during group membership that can offset any member''s income, SRLY NOLs are restricted to prevent trafficking in loss corporations by limiting the deduction to the acquired member''s own earnings contribution.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4094;

-- ID 4096: Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under Reg. Section 1.1502-21, the CNOL carryforward of $300,000 is not subject to SRLY limitations because it was generated during consolidated group membership. The 80% limitation under IRC Section 172(a) applies: 80% × $1,200,000 = $960,000, so the full $300,000 CNOL is deductible. The SRLY NOL of $200,000 is limited to Sub X''s separate contribution of $250,000 under Reg. Section 1.1502-21(c), and the 80% rule further applies to the SRLY component. After applying the CNOL, the remaining SRLY deduction is computed on Sub X''s contribution: 80% × $250,000 = $200,000, allowing the full $200,000 SRLY NOL. However, the sequential coordination of these limits produces a total deduction of approximately $440,000. Unlike CNOLs that offset any group income, SRLY NOLs are compartmentalized to the contributing member''s earnings.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4096;

-- ============================================================
-- INDIVIDUAL TAX PLANNING: COMPENSATION (3 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 3956: Individual Tax Planning: Compensation
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 409A and Revenue Procedure 92-64, a rabbi trust''s assets remain subject to the claims of the employer''s general creditors in the event of bankruptcy or insolvency. When the employer files for bankruptcy, the executive becomes an unsecured general creditor with no priority over other creditors for the deferred compensation. This creditor risk is the fundamental trade-off for deferring income recognition under Section 451 — the executive avoids current taxation only because the funds remain at substantial risk of forfeiture. Unlike a secular trust where assets are protected from creditors and the executive is taxed immediately upon contribution, a rabbi trust provides tax deferral specifically because the assets are not secured.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3956;

-- ID 12643: Individual Tax Planning: Compensation
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 164(f), a self-employed individual may deduct one-half of the self-employment tax as an above-the-line deduction in computing adjusted gross income. For Rivera with SE tax of $22,239, the deduction is $11,120 ($22,239 ÷ 2). This deduction appears on Schedule 1 (Form 1040) and reduces AGI, which in turn affects AGI-sensitive phase-outs and limitations. The deduction is not taken on Schedule C — it is a personal adjustment to income under Section 62(a)(1). Unlike the employer''s share of FICA for employees, which is deducted as a business expense by the employer, the SE tax deduction is a statutory adjustment available only to self-employed taxpayers.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12643;

-- ID 12651: Individual Tax Planning: Compensation
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 451, phantom stock units are taxed as ordinary compensation income at vesting when the cash payment is received, because no actual stock is transferred — the entire $360,000 is W-2 wages subject to FICA and income tax withholding. Since phantom stock involves no transfer of property, IRC Section 83(b) elections are not available for phantom stock. In contrast, restricted stock involves an actual transfer of property under Section 83(a), making the Section 83(b) election available to accelerate recognition to the grant date. Unlike restricted stock where the recipient can elect to be taxed on the lower grant-date value, phantom stock holders must recognize income at the potentially higher vesting-date value with no election to accelerate.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12651;

-- ============================================================
-- INTERNATIONAL TAX (3 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 4134: International Tax
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 954(d), foreign base company sales income arises when a controlled foreign corporation (CFC) buys personal property from a related person and sells it for use outside the CFC''s country of incorporation. Xi Corp''s subsidiary purchases from a related manufacturer in Country A and resells to customers in Country B, with neither country being the subsidiary''s country of incorporation — this is the classic foreign base company sales arrangement under Subpart F. The income is currently taxable to the US shareholder under Section 951(a). Unlike income from manufacturing performed by the CFC itself, which is excluded under Section 954(d)(1), pass-through sales income from a related-party arrangement triggers immediate Subpart F inclusion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4134;

-- ID 4135: International Tax
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 1291, the default PFIC rules impose no current inclusion of undistributed earnings — unlike Subpart F under Section 951 or GILTI under Section 951A, which require annual income inclusions. Tax consequences arise only upon an ''excess distribution'' (distributions exceeding 125% of the average of the prior three years'' distributions) or disposition of PFIC stock under Section 1291(a). At that point, the gain is allocated ratably over the holding period and taxed at the highest marginal rate for each year, with an interest charge added. However, a shareholder may avoid this punitive regime by making a QEF election under Section 1295 or a mark-to-market election under Section 1296.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4135;

-- ID 4136: International Tax
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 904(a), the foreign tax credit (FTC) is limited to US tax multiplied by the ratio of foreign-source taxable income to worldwide taxable income. For Omicron Corp: US tax = $3,000,000 × 21% = $630,000. FTC limitation = $630,000 × ($1,000,000 / $3,000,000) = $210,000. Since foreign taxes paid ($250,000) exceed the limitation ($210,000), the credit is capped at $210,000. The $40,000 excess credit may be carried back one year or forward ten years under Section 904(c). Unlike a deduction for foreign taxes under Section 164, the credit provides a dollar-for-dollar reduction of US tax but is subject to this proportional limitation to prevent crediting foreign taxes against US-source income.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4136;

-- ============================================================
-- MULTI-JURISDICTIONAL TAX PLANNING (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 4984: Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under state income tax conformity rules, most states that use federal taxable income as the starting point require a positive modification (addback) for state income taxes deducted on the federal return. This addback is necessary because allowing a deduction for a state''s own income tax would be circular — the tax liability would depend on the deduction, which depends on the tax liability. IRC Section 164(a)(3) permits the federal deduction, but states override this through their own addback statutes. Unlike deductions for property taxes or sales taxes that most states allow, the state income tax deduction is almost universally required to be added back in computing state taxable income.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4984;

-- ID 4985: Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Public Law 86-272 (codified at 15 U.S.C. Section 381) protects a taxpayer from state income tax when activities are limited to the solicitation of orders for sales of tangible personal property. Digital software licenses are intangible property and fall entirely outside the statute''s protection, regardless of the level of activity in the state. Because Corporation P sells only digital software licenses, P.L. 86-272 cannot shield it from State J''s income tax despite P having no physical presence. Unlike sellers of tangible goods who may limit their in-state activities to solicitation and claim protection, sellers of intangible property must look to other nexus thresholds such as economic nexus statutes to determine their filing obligations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4985;

-- ============================================================
-- NONTAXABLE DISPOSITIONS (3 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 4374: Nontaxable Dispositions
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 453, the installment method spreads gain recognition over the payment period but does not change the total gain. The gross profit is $240,000 ($400,000 sale price − $160,000 basis), and the gross profit ratio is 60% ($240,000 / $400,000). Each annual $100,000 payment triggers $60,000 of gain recognition ($100,000 × 60%), totaling $240,000 over four years. The installment method under Section 453(c) merely affects timing, not the aggregate gain recognized. Unlike an outright sale where the entire $240,000 gain is recognized in the year of sale, the installment method defers recognition to match cash receipts.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4374;

-- ID 14373: Nontaxable Dispositions
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 1033 and Reg. Section 1.1033(a)-2(c)(8), severance damages in a partial condemnation compensate the property owner for the reduction in fair market value of the retained property after the government takes a portion. Severance damages reduce the basis of the retained property rather than being treated as proceeds from the converted portion. This treatment reflects that the retained property has declined in value due to the taking. Unlike the condemnation award for the taken strip, which is treated as amount realized on an involuntary conversion, severance damages address the economic harm to property the owner still holds.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14373;

-- ID 14381: Nontaxable Dispositions
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 1033(a)(2), gain from an involuntary conversion is recognized only to the extent that the amount realized exceeds the cost of qualifying replacement property. Mendez realizes $200,000 gain ($500,000 − $300,000 basis) but reinvests only $450,000, so $50,000 is recognized and $150,000 is deferred. The total replacement basis equals $450,000 − $150,000 = $300,000 (preserving the original basis). The deferred gain is allocated proportionally: Building A = $350,000 − ($150,000 × 350/450) = $233,333, and Building B = $100,000 − ($150,000 × 100/450) = $66,667. Unlike using cost basis alone, Section 1033 reduces replacement property basis by deferred gain to ensure it is eventually recognized upon disposition.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 14381;

-- ============================================================
-- OWNER-ENTITY TRANSACTIONS (5 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 4173: Owner-Entity Transactions
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 707(c), guaranteed payments are treated as payments to a non-partner for purposes of computing partnership income, meaning they are deducted regardless of whether the partnership has income or a loss. The partnership''s ordinary loss after the $60,000 guaranteed payment is $20,000 + $60,000 = $80,000. This loss is allocated to all partners under Section 704. The partner receiving the guaranteed payment still reports $60,000 as ordinary income under Section 707(c). Unlike distributive shares under Section 704(b), which depend on partnership income, guaranteed payments are fixed and create a deduction that can increase a partnership loss.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4173;

-- ID 4175: Owner-Entity Transactions
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 1366(d)(1)(B), direct shareholder loans to an S corporation create debt basis that allows loss deductions beyond stock basis. Nancy''s $200,000 loan creates $200,000 of debt basis. Losses first reduce stock basis under Section 1367(a)(2), and once stock basis reaches zero, additional losses reduce debt basis. Importantly, only direct loans from the shareholder create debt basis — under the economic outlay doctrine established in case law, third-party loans merely guaranteed by the shareholder do not increase debt basis. Unlike partnership outside basis under Section 752 where a partner''s share of entity-level debt increases basis, S corporation basis requires an actual economic outlay by the shareholder.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4175;

-- ID 4176: Owner-Entity Transactions
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 1372(a), a greater-than-2% S corporation shareholder is treated as a partner (not an employee) for purposes of fringe benefit exclusions. This means the shareholder-employee cannot exclude employer-provided group-term life insurance premiums under Section 79. The entire $5,000 premium must be included in the shareholder-employee''s W-2 wages as compensation income, regardless of the coverage amount. Unlike regular employees who can exclude the cost of up to $50,000 of group-term life coverage under Section 79(a), greater-than-2% S corporation shareholders must include the full premium amount in gross income.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4176;

-- ID 13493: Owner-Entity Transactions
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under the IRS debt-versus-equity analysis rooted in IRC Section 385 and judicial factors, courts evaluate debt-to-equity ratio, fixed maturity, unconditional repayment obligation, and other indicia of debt. A 40:1 debt-to-equity ratio ($2,000,000 / $50,000) constitutes extreme thin capitalization, and contingent repayment based on earnings strongly indicates equity rather than bona fide debt. The loan is reclassified as equity, and interest payments become nondeductible constructive dividends. Unlike arm''s-length loans with fixed terms and unconditional repayment, shareholder advances with excessive leverage and contingent repayment lack the substance of true indebtedness and are recharacterized under the substance-over-form doctrine.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13493;

-- ID 13495: Owner-Entity Transactions
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 316 and the constructive dividend doctrine, when a C corporation provides personal use of corporate property to a controlling shareholder, the fair market value of that personal use is treated as a constructive dividend to the extent of corporate earnings and profits (E&P). The personal-use portion is 80% × $15,000 = $12,000, which is dividend income to Marcus. The corporation loses the deduction for that portion under Section 162(a), which requires expenses to be ordinary and necessary business expenses. Unlike legitimate business use that is fully deductible to the corporation, personal use by a shareholder results in a nondeductible constructive dividend creating potential double taxation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 13495;

-- ============================================================
-- PASSIVE ACTIVITY AND AT-RISK RULES (2 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 3989: Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 469(c)(7), a taxpayer qualifies as a real estate professional if they meet two requirements: (1) more than 750 hours of services in real property trades or businesses, and (2) more than half of total personal services are performed in real property trades or businesses. The real estate broker satisfies both tests with 1,200 hours in brokerage activities exceeding the 750-hour threshold and representing more than half of total personal services. The taxpayer must also materially participate in each rental activity under Section 469(c)(7)(B). Unlike taxpayers who merely invest in rental properties, qualifying real estate professionals can treat rental activities as nonpassive, allowing losses to offset other income.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3989;

-- ID 3990: Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 469(i), active participants in rental real estate activities may deduct up to $25,000 of rental losses against nonpassive income. This special allowance phases out by $1 for every $2 of AGI exceeding $100,000 under Section 469(i)(3)(A). With AGI of $110,000, the excess is $10,000, reducing the allowance by $5,000 ($10,000 ÷ 2) to $20,000. Since the $18,000 rental loss is less than the $20,000 available allowance, the entire $18,000 is deductible. Unlike passive losses that are fully suspended under the general Section 469 rules, the special allowance provides active rental participants a limited offset against nonpassive income up to the phase-out threshold.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3990;

-- ============================================================
-- RETIREMENT PLANS AND EDUCATION SAVINGS (9 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 4054: Retirement Plans and Education Savings
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 72(t)(4), if a substantially equal periodic payment (SEPP) plan is modified before the later of five years from the first distribution or the taxpayer reaching age 59½, the 10% early withdrawal penalty under Section 72(t)(1) is imposed retroactively on all prior distributions received under the plan, plus interest from the original due date of each year''s return. At age 45 with only three years of payments, the taxpayer has not satisfied the five-year requirement, and the modification triggers the recapture penalty. Unlike early withdrawals that incur a one-time 10% penalty, a SEPP modification creates retroactive liability on all previously penalty-free distributions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4054;

-- ID 4055: Retirement Plans and Education Savings
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 529(e)(3), qualified higher education expenses for 529 plan purposes include tuition, fees, books, supplies, and equipment required for enrollment or attendance at an eligible educational institution. Room and board (subject to cost-of-attendance limits), computers, and related technology are also qualified expenses. The SECURE Act added up to $10,000 in aggregate for student loan repayment under Section 529(c)(9). However, transportation costs, health insurance premiums, and general living expenses are not qualified 529 expenses. Unlike Coverdell ESA expenses under Section 530, which cover K-12 supplies broadly, Section 529 qualified expenses at the college level focus specifically on costs directly related to enrollment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4055;

-- ID 4056: Retirement Plans and Education Savings
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 408A(c)(3), Roth IRA contribution eligibility phases out for married filing jointly taxpayers based on modified AGI. At $250,000 MAGI, the couple exceeds the phase-out range and cannot make direct Roth IRA contributions. However, no income limit applies to Roth conversions under Section 408A(d)(3), enabling the ''backdoor Roth'' strategy: make a nondeductible traditional IRA contribution under Section 408(o) and then convert to a Roth IRA. The pro-rata rule under Section 408(d)(2) may apply if the taxpayer has other pre-tax IRA balances. Unlike direct contributions that are income-limited, Roth conversions are available to all taxpayers regardless of income level.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4056;

-- ID 4057: Retirement Plans and Education Savings
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 529(c)(3)(C)(ii), changing the beneficiary of a 529 plan to a member of the original beneficiary''s family as defined in Section 529(e)(2) is not treated as a distribution and triggers no income tax, penalty, or gift tax consequences. Family members include siblings, parents, children, spouses, nieces, nephews, and first cousins. Since Karen''s younger daughter is a sibling of the original beneficiary, the change is a tax-free transfer. Unlike a withdrawal of funds for nonqualified purposes, which triggers income tax and a 10% penalty on the earnings portion, a beneficiary change within the family preserves the plan''s tax-advantaged status entirely.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4057;

-- ID 4059: Retirement Plans and Education Savings
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 408(p)(2)(B), when an employer elects the 2% nonelective contribution for a SIMPLE IRA plan, the employer must contribute 2% of each eligible employee''s compensation regardless of whether the employee makes elective deferrals. For an employee earning $60,000, the required contribution is $60,000 × 2% = $1,200. The compensation cap under Section 408(p)(2)(B)(ii) applies (approximately $350,000 for 2026). Unlike the matching contribution option under Section 408(p)(2)(A), which requires employer contributions only when the employee defers, the nonelective option mandates contributions for all eligible employees regardless of participation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4059;

-- ID 4061: Retirement Plans and Education Savings
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 408(j), the maximum SEP IRA contribution is the lesser of 25% of the employee''s compensation or the dollar limit ($70,000 for 2026, as adjusted under Section 415(d)). For Dr. Greenfield with $350,000 compensation, 25% equals $87,500, but the Section 415(c)(1)(A) dollar limit caps the contribution at $70,000. The Section 401(a)(17) compensation cap (approximately $350,000 for 2026) limits the compensation used in the calculation, but here the dollar ceiling is the binding constraint. Unlike defined benefit plans under Section 415(b) where the limit is based on annual benefit amounts, SEP IRAs are defined contribution plans subject to the Section 415(c) annual additions limit.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4061;

-- ID 4062: Retirement Plans and Education Savings
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 4974 as amended by SECURE 2.0 Act Section 302, the excise tax for failing to take the full required minimum distribution was reduced from 50% to 25% of the shortfall. The shortfall is $25,000 − $15,000 = $10,000, producing an excise tax of 25% × $10,000 = $2,500. If the taxpayer corrects the shortfall within the correction window under Section 4974(d) (generally by the end of the second tax year following the year of the missed RMD), the penalty is further reduced to 10%. Unlike the pre-SECURE 2.0 regime that imposed a flat 50% penalty, the current rules provide graduated penalties that incentivize timely correction of RMD shortfalls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4062;

-- ID 4064: Retirement Plans and Education Savings
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 529(c)(7), added by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act, 529 plan distributions for tuition at elementary and secondary schools are tax-free but limited to $10,000 per student per year. Of the $12,000 distribution, $10,000 qualifies as a tax-free distribution for K-12 tuition, and the remaining $2,000 is a nonqualified distribution. Under Section 529(c)(6), the earnings portion of the $2,000 excess is subject to income tax and a 10% additional tax. Unlike higher education expenses where there is no annual dollar cap on qualified distributions, K-12 tuition distributions are strictly limited to $10,000 annually per beneficiary.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4064;

-- ID 4065: Retirement Plans and Education Savings
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 408A(d)(2), a qualified distribution from a Roth IRA is entirely tax-free and penalty-free when two conditions are met: (1) the five-year holding period under Section 408A(d)(2)(B) has been satisfied, and (2) the distribution is made after age 59½, death, disability, or for a first-time home purchase. At age 62 with a six-year-old account, both requirements are met, so the entire $50,000 — including $20,000 of earnings — is received tax-free. Unlike traditional IRA distributions that are taxed as ordinary income under Section 408(d)(1), qualified Roth distributions exclude both contributions and accumulated earnings from gross income.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4065;

-- ============================================================
-- TRUSTS AND ESTATES (4 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 4267: Trusts and Estates
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 652(b), distributions from a trust carry out the proportionate character of distributable net income (DNI) to the beneficiary. Qualified dividends comprise $20,000 of $50,000 total DNI (40%). When the trust distributes $25,000, the qualified dividend portion is 40% × $25,000 = $10,000, and the remaining $15,000 carries out as ordinary interest income. This proportional allocation preserves the character of income as it passes through the trust to the beneficiary. Unlike a conduit entity that passes through specific items, trust distributions allocate each type of income proportionally based on its share of DNI.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4267;

-- ID 4268: Trusts and Estates
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 643(a)(3), capital gains allocated to corpus under the governing instrument or applicable local law are generally excluded from distributable net income (DNI). Since this trust''s instrument allocates capital gains to corpus, the $50,000 long-term capital gain is excluded from DNI and taxed at the trust level at the applicable capital gains rate. Capital gains are included in DNI only when allocated to income, actually distributed to beneficiaries, or used for charitable purposes under Section 643(a)(3). Unlike ordinary income that flows through DNI to beneficiaries, capital gains allocated to corpus are typically taxed at the compressed trust tax brackets.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4268;

-- ID 4270: Trusts and Estates
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 1411(a)(2), the 3.8% net investment income tax (NIIT) applies to estates and trusts when undistributed net investment income exceeds the dollar amount at which the highest trust tax bracket begins — approximately $15,200 for 2025 (adjusted annually for inflation under Section 1(e)). This threshold is dramatically lower than the individual thresholds of $200,000 for single filers and $250,000 for married filing jointly under Section 1411(b). Trusts are particularly vulnerable to the NIIT because the compressed trust brackets reach the highest rate at a very low income level. Unlike individuals who can earn substantial investment income before triggering NIIT, trusts face the tax on nearly all undistributed investment income.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4270;

-- ID 4271: Trusts and Estates
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 652(b), income distributed from a trust retains its character in the hands of the beneficiary. The beneficiary includes only the $15,000 of taxable interest income in gross income. The $5,000 of tax-exempt interest retains its exempt character under Section 103 when distributed and is reported on Schedule K-1 (Form 1041) for informational purposes, as it may affect calculations such as the investment interest limitation under Section 163(d). Unlike taxable income items that increase the beneficiary''s gross income, tax-exempt interest passes through without creating a tax liability while still being relevant for certain ancillary computations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4271;

