-- WEALTH TRANSFER AND GIFTING STRATEGIES (5 questions)
-- ============================================================

-- ID 4020: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 2611, there are three types of generation-skipping transfers (GSTs): direct skips under Section 2612(c) (transfers directly to skip persons), taxable distributions under Section 2612(b) (distributions from a trust to skip persons), and taxable terminations under Section 2612(a) (when the interest of the last non-skip beneficiary in a trust terminates). A gift from a parent to a child is not a GST because the child is only one generation below the transferor under Section 2651 and therefore is not a ''skip person'' as defined in Section 2613. Unlike transfers to grandchildren or more remote descendants, transfers to children fall within the normal generational hierarchy and are subject only to gift tax, not GST tax.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4020;

-- ID 4024: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 2513, married couples may elect gift splitting on Form 709, treating each gift as made one-half by each spouse. The $100,000 gift is split to $50,000 per spouse. Each spouse then applies their annual exclusion under Section 2503(b) ($19,000 for 2026), resulting in a taxable gift of $31,000 per spouse ($50,000 − $19,000). Both spouses must consent to gift splitting and both must file Form 709 for the election year. Without gift splitting, the donor spouse would have a taxable gift of $81,000 ($100,000 − $19,000). Unlike community property states where gifts of community property are automatically treated as made half by each spouse, gift splitting under Section 2513 requires an affirmative election.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4024;

-- ID 4025: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 2702, the taxable gift for a qualified personal residence trust (QPRT) is the present value of the remainder interest at the time of the transfer — here, $450,000. The annual exclusion under Section 2503(b) does not apply because a remainder interest is a future interest, not a present interest. If the grantor survives the 10-year retained term, the residence passes to the remainder beneficiaries and is excluded from the grantor''s gross estate under Section 2036, effectively transferring a $1,200,000+ asset for a $450,000 gift tax cost. Unlike an outright gift where the full fair market value is the taxable gift reduced by the annual exclusion, a QPRT leverages the time value of money to significantly discount the transfer tax cost.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4025;

-- ID 4026: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 2503(b), only present interests qualify for the annual gift tax exclusion. Gifts to an irrevocable trust are generally future interests that do not qualify. To convert them to present interests, the trust must include Crummey withdrawal powers — named after the landmark case Crummey v. Commissioner (397 F.2d 82, 9th Cir. 1968) — giving beneficiaries a temporary right (typically 30 to 60 days) to withdraw newly contributed amounts. The powers must be real and beneficiaries must receive written notice of their withdrawal rights. Unlike direct gifts to individuals that automatically qualify as present interests, gifts to trusts require these demand powers to satisfy the present-interest requirement of Section 2503(b).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4026;

-- ID 4029: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Under IRC Section 664(d)(2), a charitable remainder unitrust (CRUT) pays a fixed percentage of the trust assets as revalued annually, so payments fluctuate with trust investment performance. Under Section 664(d)(1), a charitable remainder annuity trust (CRAT) pays a fixed dollar annuity determined at inception that does not change regardless of trust value. Additionally, Section 664(d)(2)(B) permits additional contributions to a CRUT after initial funding, whereas Section 664(d)(1) prohibits additional contributions to a CRAT. Both must meet the 10% remainder value test under Section 664(d)(2)(D) and the minimum 5% payout requirement. Unlike a CRAT where payments remain constant, a CRUT''s variable payments provide a natural inflation hedge when trust assets appreciate.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4029;

COMMIT;
